  "narration": {
    "title": "Kurtz Catches Fire, Schwarber Keeps Counting",
    "intro": "The bats are doing the talking this week. Yordan Alvarez still owns the top OPS in the game, but the loudest week belonged to a few sluggers who spent the weekend rounding the bases. Here's who's hot and who cooled off.",
    "offense_lead": "The Dodgers lead the league in team OPS at .789, with the Yankees and Braves right behind. The Nationals sneak into fourth, proof that James Wood isn't doing it alone.",
    "bats_lead": "Alvarez sits atop the OPS list at 1.066, a hair off where he was a week ago. Behind him, Nick Kurtz climbed to .967 with a four-homer week, while Kyle Schwarber quietly piled up to a league-best 24 home runs.",
    "hr_lead": "Kurtz did his damage in a hurry: one against Houston, then three across two days against Milwaukee. Byron Buxton matched the pace with three of his own, including a back-to-back finish against Detroit, while Brandon Marsh homered on three straight days against the White Sox."
  }
